## Write your first configuration

Create `main.tf`:

```hcl
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = { source = "hashicorp/aws", version = "~> 5.0" }
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "notes" {
  bucket = "my-unique-notes-bucket-12345"
}
```

## Run the core workflow

Then run the core workflow:

```bash
terraform init     # download providers
terraform plan     # preview
terraform apply    # create
terraform destroy  # clean up
```

**Practice:** Create and destroy a bucket, then read the generated `terraform.tfstate`.

## Inputs, outputs and reuse

Hard-coded values do not scale. Use inputs and outputs:

```hcl
variable "env" {
  type    = string
  default = "dev"
}

locals {
  name = "app-${var.env}"
}

output "bucket_arn" {
  value = aws_s3_bucket.notes.arn
}
```

Set values with `-var env=prod`, a `terraform.tfvars` file, or `TF_VAR_env` environment variables.

## Create many similar resources

Use `count` and `for_each` to create many similar resources:

```hcl
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "logs" {
  for_each = toset(["web", "api"])
  bucket   = "${local.name}-${each.key}-logs"
}
```

## Configuration and secrets

Keep config out of images:

```yaml
envFrom:
  - configMapRef: { name: app-config }
  - secretRef: { name: app-secrets }
readinessProbe:
  httpGet: { path: /health, port: 8080 }
livenessProbe:
  httpGet: { path: /health, port: 8080 }
```

## Health checks

Readiness controls traffic; liveness restarts stuck containers. For production secrets use External Secrets with a cloud secret manager.

## Install and run Java

Install a JDK 21 (Temurin) and verify with `java -version`. Java 21 runs single files directly:

```java
// Hello.java
void main() {
    System.out.println("Hello, Java 21");
}
```

## Run a single file

```bash
java --enable-preview --source 21 Hello.java
```

Java is statically typed, compiled to bytecode and runs on the JVM.

## Records and sealed types

```java
public sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Square {}
public record Circle(double r) implements Shape {}
public record Square(double side) implements Shape {}

static double area(Shape s) {
    return switch (s) {
        case Circle c -> Math.PI * c.r() * c.r();
        case Square q -> q.side() * q.side();
    };
}
```

## Why these types help

Records are immutable data carriers; sealed types plus pattern matching give exhaustive switches.

## Virtual threads

Java 21 virtual threads make blocking I/O cheap:

```java
try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    IntStream.range(0, 10_000).forEach(i ->
        executor.submit(() -> { Thread.sleep(Duration.ofSeconds(1)); return i; }));
}
```

## What this buys you

Ten thousand concurrent tasks without a reactive framework.

## Promises and async code

```js
import { readFile } from 'node:fs/promises';

async function main() {
  const [a, b] = await Promise.all([
    readFile('a.txt', 'utf8'),
    readFile('b.txt', 'utf8'),
  ]);
  console.log(a.length + b.length);
}
main().catch(console.error);
```

## Run work in parallel

Use `Promise.all` to start independent reads together and wait for both results.

## A small HTTP server

```js
import express from 'express';
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());
app.get('/health', (_req, res) => res.json({ status: 'UP' }));
app.listen(3000);
```

## Beyond a small server

For larger apps use a structured framework such as NestJS (see the TypeScript backend roadmap).

## Interfaces and errors

```go
type Store interface {
    Get(id string) (string, error)
}

var ErrNotFound = errors.New("not found")

func load(s Store, id string) error {
    if _, err := s.Get(id); err != nil {
        return fmt.Errorf("load %s: %w", id, err)
    }
    return nil
}
```

## Rules to remember

Interfaces are satisfied implicitly; errors are values, wrapped with `%w`.

## A REST controller

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/books")
class BookController {
    record BookRequest(@NotBlank String title) {}

    @PostMapping
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.CREATED)
    Book create(@Valid @RequestBody BookRequest r) { return service.create(r); }
}
```

## Keep controllers thin

Put business logic in `@Service` classes and keep controllers thin.

## Repositories

```java
public interface BookRepository extends JpaRepository<Book, Long> {
    List<Book> findByTitleContainingIgnoreCase(String q);
}
```

## Schema changes with Flyway

Manage the schema with **Flyway** migrations (`db/migration/V1__init.sql`) rather than `ddl-auto`. Watch for N+1 queries and use `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` for reads.

## Types you will use

```ts
interface Lesson { id: number; title: string; tags?: string[] }
type Level = 'BEGINNER' | 'ADVANCED';

function first<T>(items: T[]): T | undefined {
  return items[0];
}
```

## Strict mode and advanced types

Enable `strict` mode. Learn unions, generics, narrowing and utility types (`Partial`, `Pick`, `Record`).

## Controllers and modules

NestJS uses decorators and dependency injection:

```ts
@Controller('lessons')
export class LessonsController {
  constructor(private readonly service: LessonsService) {}

  @Get(':id')
  find(@Param('id', ParseIntPipe) id: number) {
    return this.service.find(id);
  }
}
```

## Organize by feature

Group features into `@Module`s.

Tables, rows, primary keys and foreign keys. Normalize to remove duplication, and denormalize deliberately for read performance.

```sql
CREATE TABLE topics (
  id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  name TEXT NOT NULL
);
CREATE TABLE lessons (
  id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  topic_id BIGINT REFERENCES topics(id),
  title TEXT NOT NULL
);
```

Organize data as **Bronze** (raw) > **Silver** (cleaned) > **Gold** (business aggregates).

```sql
CREATE TABLE silver.events AS SELECT * FROM bronze.events WHERE user_id IS NOT NULL;
OPTIMIZE silver.events ZORDER BY (user_id);
SELECT * FROM silver.events VERSION AS OF 3;  -- time travel
```

```sql
CREATE WAREHOUSE learn_wh WAREHOUSE_SIZE = XSMALL AUTO_SUSPEND = 60;
CREATE DATABASE learn;
CREATE STAGE learn.public.raw;
-- upload files with PUT, then:
COPY INTO learn.public.events FROM @learn.public.raw FILE_FORMAT = (TYPE = JSON);
```

For continuous loading, use **Snowpipe** or Snowpipe Streaming.

**Snowpark** runs Python, Java and Scala next to the data. **Cortex AI** brings LLMs to SQL:

```sql
SELECT AI_COMPLETE('claude-3-5-sonnet', 'Summarize: ' || review) FROM reviews;
SELECT AI_SENTIMENT(review) FROM reviews;
```

Cortex Search powers RAG, and Cortex Analyst answers questions in natural language over semantic views.
